abstract: >-
  The Thiele Machine is a model of computation with a price in the step
  where something settles and stays settled. A Thiele machine is a
  thing, moves that take it to the next thing, a reading of how settled
  it is that no move lowers, some run from a chosen start that raises
  the reading, prices that are nothing or more, and one rule, the toll:
  a move that settles something more than it was costs at least one. It
  computes nothing a Turing machine cannot; every machine that steps is
  a Thiele machine with its record covered and the price of settling set
  to zero. The monograph, a second edition written from the start,
  argues every result on the page with the reader: keeping a settled
  answer takes two states landing on one or an endless supply of fresh
  states; with finitely many states, settled staying settled and a price
  on merging, the toll follows; a window that hides the record cannot
  price exactly; a box that answers only to the machine and never comes
  down is a latch on one event; a machine that reads an established
  record to skip work pays in one of four ways, and under two stated
  conditions there is no fifth; exact counts of the room keeping takes.
  Heat results are conditional on Landauer's principle, an assumption
  with its critics named. Every claim is sorted into shown, chosen,
  assumed, cited, thesis, or not claimed; the pointer thesis and the
  Thiele thesis are theses. A companion document gives the exact
  statements. The publication pairs the monograph with the matching
  tagged repository snapshot, which carries a Python check for every
  chapter that counts something and a formal check of the same
  statements, kept as tools for catching mistakes. The software is
  licensed Apache-2.0. The monograph and documentation are licensed
  CC-BY-SA-4.0.
